How to read the known world:
- Under a warp, "leads to X:" is what you found beyond that exit the last time you used it — memory, not what you see now. It only lists what is reachable from where that exit drops you, so two exits to the same map can lead to different parts of it.
- Lines starting with ">" are exits inside that place; each extra indentation is one more exit away. To reach something nested, take the numbered warp at the top of that branch.
- 🆕 NOT VISITED: you never used that exit, so you don't know where it leads. Taking it is how you discover new places.
- (visited): you used it before, but it is too far away to show what is behind it.
- Quoted text is what that person or sign told you last time; 🆕 NOT INTERACTED means you never talked to or checked it.
- 🚧 BLOCKS THE WAY to: that person or object stands in the path to what is listed; it is out of reach until it moves or is picked up.
- Exits that just lead back to where you are now are left out.
- You can only act on the numbered INTERACT/GO options of the current map.

